The C program is tiny — read one float with [38;5;10mscanf("%f")[0m, then dump the raw 4 bytes of the [38;5;10mfloat[0m object as lowercase hex plus a newline — but nearly all of the behavior lives inside [38;5;10mscanf[0m, so that is what [38;5;10mtranslation/src/main.rs[0m reimplements: whitespace skipping, longest-prefix token matching, decimal and C99 hex forms, [38;5;10minf[0m/[38;5;10mnan[0m spellings, and round-to-nearest-even rounding of hex significands into [38;5;10mf32[0m. Bytes are emitted with [38;5;10mto_ne_bytes()[0m so the dump's endianness follows the machine, as the C cast does. On a match failure or EOF nothing is assigned and [38;5;10mx[0m keeps its initial [38;5;10m0.f[0m, printing [38;5;10m00000000[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Differential testing surfaced three glibc [38;5;10mscanf[0m quirks that a straightforward [38;5;10mstrtof[0m-based translation gets wrong, all of which are reproduced deliberately rather than "fixed":[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- Once [38;5;10minf[0m is read and the next character is another [38;5;10mi[0m, glibc commits to [38;5;10minfinity[0m and reports a match failure if the rest doesn't follow. So [38;5;10minfin[0m prints [38;5;10m00000000[0m, not infinity.[0m[0m
- glibc buffers the token with the sign stripped off and rejects a buffer that is exactly [38;5;10m0x[0m, so [38;5;10m-0x[0m prints [38;5;10m+0[0m ([38;5;10m00000000[0m).[0m[0m
- A radix point makes that buffer [38;5;10m0x.[0m, which goes to [38;5;10mstrtof[0m and converts the leading [38;5;10m0[0m, so the sign survives: [38;5;10m-0x.[0m prints [38;5;10m-0[0m ([38;5;10m00000080[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Coverage included exact [38;5;10mf32[0m round-trips, 25-bit ties, hex mantissas long enough to exercise the sticky-bit path, the subnormal and overflow boundaries, partial [38;5;10minf[0m/[38;5;10mnan(...)[0m spellings, malformed tokens, and random byte garbage. One thing I did not replicate exactly: the Rust version reads stdin byte-by-byte with lookahead rather than mimicking glibc's pushback limit. That is unobservable here because the program exits immediately after the single read, but it would matter if a second read were ever added.[0m[0m
